    id: 1337,
    module: 2,
    domain: "Information and Ideas",
    passage: "The debate over 'nature versus nurture' has been one of the longest-running controversies in psychology. The nature perspective emphasizes the role of genetic inheritance in shaping behavior, personality, and cognitive abilities. The nurture perspective emphasizes the role of environment, upbringing, and experience. For much of the twentieth century, the debate was polarized, with behaviorists such as B. F. Skinner arguing that the environment was everything and that any child could be trained to become any type of specialist. Meanwhile, genetic researchers pointed to the striking similarities between identical twins raised apart as evidence for the powerful influence of heredity. Modern research, however, has largely moved beyond this dichotomy. The emerging consensus is that nature and nurture interact in complex ways. Genes influence the environments that people seek out (a phenomenon called 'gene-environment correlation'), and environmental factors can affect gene expression through epigenetic mechanisms. The question is no longer which factor matters more, but how they work together to shape development.",
