    id: 1339,
    module: 2,
    domain: "Information and Ideas",
    passage: "The concept of 'moral hazard' originated in the insurance industry but has since been applied to economics and public policy. Moral hazard occurs when one party takes on excessive risk because they know that they will not bear the full consequences of that risk. For example, a driver with comprehensive car insurance may drive more recklessly than one without insurance, knowing that the insurance company will pay for damages. In economics, the term is often used to describe the behavior of banks that take excessive risks because they expect government bailouts if their bets go wrong. The 2008 financial crisis was widely attributed to moral hazard, as banks made risky mortgage loans knowing that they were 'too big to fail' and would be rescued by the government. Critics of the moral hazard framework argue that it oversimplifies complex decision-making and ignores the genuine fear of loss that even insured parties experience. Nonetheless, the concept remains influential in shaping regulations designed to ensure that parties bear the consequences of their own risky behavior.",
