    id: 1341,
    module: 2,
    domain: "Information and Ideas",
    passage: "The concept of 'the tragedy of the commons,' as described by ecologist Garrett Hardin, illustrates how shared resources can be depleted when individuals act in their own rational self-interest. Hardin used the example of a common pasture shared by multiple herders. Each herder, seeking to maximize their own profit, adds more cattle to the pasture. The benefit of each additional cow accrues entirely to the individual herder, while the cost of overgrazing is shared among all herders. Since each herder follows this same logic, the pasture is eventually destroyed. Hardin argued that this dynamic explains a wide range of environmental problems, from overfishing to air pollution. However, later scholars, most notably Elinor Ostrom, challenged Hardin's conclusion that common resources inevitably face destruction. Ostrom documented numerous examples of communities around the world that have successfully managed shared resources—such as irrigation systems, fisheries, and forests—through collective governance arrangements, without requiring either privatization or government control.",
