    passage: "The concept of 'the banality of evil,' introduced by political theorist Hannah Arendt in her 1963 book 'Eichmann in Jerusalem,' has been one of the most influential and controversial ideas in modern moral philosophy. Arendt was covering the trial of Adolf Eichmann, a Nazi official who had been instrumental in organizing the logistics of the Holocaust. What struck Arendt was not Eichmann's monstrosity but his ordinariness. He was not a fanatical ideologue or a sadistic monster, she argued, but a bureaucrat who followed orders and was motivated by career advancement rather than deep-seated hatred. Arendt coined the phrase 'the banality of evil' to describe how ordinary people can commit horrific acts not out of malevolence but through thoughtless conformity, bureaucratic obedience, and the failure to think critically about the consequences of their actions. The concept was controversial from the start, with critics arguing that Arendt had underestimated Eichmann's genuine anti-Semitism and had been misled by his courtroom performance. Despite the controversy, the idea that evil can arise from unthinking conformity rather than pathological hatred has become a central theme in discussions of genocide, organizational ethics, and moral responsibility.",
