    passage: "The invention of the elevator safety brake by Elisha Otis in 1852 was a pivotal moment in the development of modern cities. Before Otis's invention, elevators were considered dangerous because if the hoisting cable broke, the elevator car would fall. Otis demonstrated his safety brake dramatically at the 1854 New York World's Fair: he stood on an elevator platform, had the hoisting cable cut, and the platform fell only a few inches before the safety brake engaged. This invention made passenger elevators practical and safe, which in turn made tall buildings feasible. Without safe elevators, buildings taller than about six stories—the maximum that most people were willing to climb—would not have been practical. The skyscraper, the defining architectural form of the modern city, was made possible by Otis's invention. The elevator also transformed social dynamics within buildings, as upper floors—once the least desirable because of the climb—became the most prestigious and expensive.",
