    passage: "The phenomenon of 'learned helplessness' was first systematically studied by psychologist Martin Seligman in the 1960s. In a classic experiment, dogs were placed in a chamber and given mild electric shocks from which they could not escape. Later, the dogs were placed in a different chamber where they could escape the shocks by jumping over a low barrier. The dogs that had experienced the inescapable shocks did not attempt to escape—they simply lay down and accepted the shocks. In contrast, dogs that had not experienced the prior inescapable shocks quickly learned to jump over the barrier. Seligman concluded that the dogs had learned that their actions were ineffective and had generalized this helplessness to a new situation where escape was actually possible. This research has been applied to understanding human depression: people who experience repeated failures or traumatic events may come to believe that their actions have no effect on outcomes, leading to passivity and giving up. Critics of the theory argue that it oversimplifies human depression, which involves complex biological, cognitive, and social factors that cannot be reduced to learned helplessness alone.",
